The 'stalled' event should be triggered when the media resource is unable to continue playback due to lack of data. However, there are cases where this event might not be fired as expected, especially when dealing with adaptive streaming technologies like DASH. To handle this situation, you can implement a custom logic to detect when the video is buffering.

One approach is to monitor the 'timeupdate' event and compare the current time with the buffered time range. If the current time exceeds the end of the buffered range, you can assume that the video is buffering and display a visual indicator. When new data arrives and the buffered range expands, you can remove the indicator.

