You can use a custom BeanPostProcessor to validate your properties during bean creation. Here is an example:

```java
@Component
public class PropertyValidator implements BeanPostProcessor {

    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) throws BeansException {
        if (bean instanceof Service) {
            Service service = (Service) bean;
            if (service.getProperty() == null || service.getProperty().isEmpty()) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Service property is empty or null");
            }
        }
        return bean;
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) throws BeansException {
        return bean;
    }
}
```

This custom BeanPostProcessor will check the `property` of the `Service` bean before initialization. If the property is empty or null, it will throw an `IllegalArgumentException` with a readable message. This will prevent the application from starting up, as the bean creation will fail. This approach keeps your Service class clean and does not require any additional code in your setters. It also allows you to easily add more validation rules if needed.

