samples.guided_completion:
The file_put_contents() function is atomic, but the file system operations it relies on are not. This means that if a client is reading the file at the same time as another is writing to it, the reader may get a file that is incomplete or in a corrupted state.

The solution is to write to a temporary file, then rename it to the final name. The rename operation is atomic, so there will never be a point in time when a client can see a partially written file.

Here's a simple example:

$tmpfile = tempnam('/tmp', 'index');
file_put_contents($tmpfile, $content);
rename($tmpfile, '/path/to/index.html');

This will ensure that all clients always get a complete, uncorrupted file. If the writing process is interrupted for any reason, the old version of the file will remain in place until the next successful write.

